<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">War and march · Anthem · 1840 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">Die Wacht am Rhein</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A German song of 1840 calling on Germans to guard the river Rhine against France. It was sung in Germany's wars until 1945.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Max Schneckenburger</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Karl Wilhelm</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>German nationalists of the 19th century; later the German Empire</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Switzerland (the poem); Krefeld, Prussia (Karl Wilhelm's music)</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>poem 1840; music 1854</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>War and march · Anthem</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words by Max Schneckenburger (1840; d. 1847), music by Karl Wilhelm (1854))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>The song grew out of the long enmity between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> over the left bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, which French armies had occupied in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nine-years-war">Nine Years' War</a> and again in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars">Napoleonic Wars</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine-crisis">Rhine Crisis of 1840</a> the French prime minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolphe-thiers">Adolphe Thiers</a> claimed the Upper and Middle Rhine as France's &quot;natural eastern border&quot;, setting off a movement of Rhine songs in Germany. Inspired by Nikolaus Becker's successful &quot;Der Deutsche Rhein&quot;, the Swabian merchant Max Schneckenburger wrote a poem of five stanzas, first called &quot;Rheinwache&quot;; two more stanzas were added later by others. Like the &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deutschlandlied">Deutschlandlied</a>&quot; and Ernst Moritz Arndt's &quot;Was ist des Deutschen Vaterland?&quot;, and unlike &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heil-dir-im-siegerkranz">Heil dir im Siegerkranz</a>&quot;, which praised a monarch, it called on Germans to set aside the rivalries of their many states and unite.</p><p>Schneckenburger worked in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>, and the poem was first set to music in Bern by the organist J. Mendel and sung by the tenor Adolph Methfessel for the Prussian ambassador; that version did not catch on. In 1854, seven years after Schneckenburger's death, Karl Wilhelm, music director of Krefeld, set the poem again and performed it with his men's chorus on 11 June, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> wedding anniversary of Prince Wilhelm of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, the future Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelm-i">Wilhelm I</a>. This setting became popular after it was sung at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dresden">Dresden</a>.</p><p>The song became famous in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franco-prussian-war">Franco-Prussian War</a> of 1870–1871. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a> that followed, which included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace">Alsace</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a>, it stood beside &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heil-dir-im-siegerkranz">Heil dir im Siegerkranz</a>&quot; as an unofficial second national anthem, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bismarck-battleship">Bismarck</a> granted the composer and the author's family an annual pension. Its words appear on the Niederwald monument of 1883 above <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudesheim-am-rhein">Rüdesheim am Rhein</a>. From the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> to 1945 it was one of the most popular songs in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, rivalling the &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deutschlandlied">Deutschlandlied</a>&quot; as the de facto anthem. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wehrmacht">Wehrmacht</a>'s daily radio report opened with its tune until 1941, and its title was the code name of the German offensive of 1944 now known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-bulge">Battle of the Bulge</a>. The invasion across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> that the song imagined never came as it pictured: the fighting of 1870–1871, 1914–1918 and 1940 took place in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, and when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> crossed the river in Operation Plunder in 1945 Germany was near collapse.</p><p>The song figures in stage works and films. In Lewis Milestone's film All Quiet on the Western Front (1930) it accompanies schoolboys leaving their classroom to enlist; in Jean Renoir's La Grande Illusion (1937) and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/casablanca">Casablanca</a> (1942) it is sung by Germans and drowned out by &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-marseillaise">La Marseillaise</a>&quot;. Lillian Hellman took its title for her play Watch on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> (1941). Its tune was also borrowed for gentler ends: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ial">Yale</a>'s &quot;Bright College Years&quot; (1881) and the school song of Doshisha University. After the Élysée Treaty of 1963 ended the French–German enmity, the song kept only historical significance in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, where it is now rarely sung or played.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A call to all Germans to hurry to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> and defend it, presenting the river as German and imagining the nation's youth rising together to keep any enemy from its banks. The mood is martial and defiant, a rallying song of the age of national unification.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">German</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-2"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">German</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Es braust ein Ruf wie Donnerhall,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>A call roars like a peal of thunder,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Wie Schwertgeklirr und Wogenprall:</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>like the clash of swords and the crash of waves:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Zum Rhein, zum Rhein, zum deutschen Rhein!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>To the Rhine, to the Rhine, to the German Rhine!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Wer will des Stromes Hüter sein?</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Who will be the river's guardian?</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Lieb’ Vaterland, magst ruhig sein,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Dear Fatherland, you may rest easy,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Fest steht und treu die Wacht, die Wacht am Rhein!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>firm and true stands the watch, the watch on the Rhine!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Durch Hunderttausend zuckt es schnell,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Through hundreds of thousands it flashes quickly,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Und Aller Augen blitzen hell,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and the eyes of all flash bright;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Der deutsche Jüngling, fromm und stark,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the German youth, pious and strong,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Beschirmt die heil’ge Landesmark.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>shields the holy border land.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Er blickt hinauf in Himmelsau’n,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>He looks up into the meadows of heaven,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Wo Heldengeister niederschau’n,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>where heroes' spirits look down,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Und schwört mit stolzer Kampfeslust:</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and swears with proud lust for battle:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">„Du Rhein bleibst deutsch wie meine Brust.“</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>“You, Rhine, stay German like my breast.”</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">„Und ob mein Herz im Tode bricht,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>“And though my heart break in death,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Wirst du doch drum ein Welscher nicht;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>you will not become a Welshman for all that;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Reich wie an Wasser deine Flut</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>as rich as your flood is in water</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Ist Deutschland ja an Heldenblut.“</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>is Germany indeed in heroes' blood.”</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">„Solang ein Tropfen Blut noch glüht,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>“As long as a drop of blood still glows,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Noch eine Faust den Degen zieht,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>a fist still draws the sword,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Und noch ein Arm die Büchse spannt,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and an arm still cocks the rifle,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Betritt kein Feind hier deinen Strand.“</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>no enemy will set foot here on your shore.”</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Der Schwur erschallt, die Woge rinnt,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The oath rings out, the wave runs on,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Die Fahnen flattern hoch im Wind:</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the flags flutter high in the wind:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Zum Rhein, zum Rhein, zum deutschen Rhein!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>To the Rhine, to the Rhine, to the German Rhine!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Wir Alle wollen Hüter sein!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>We all will be guardians!</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Max Schneckenburger (1840). Text from <a href="https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Die_Wacht_am_Rhein" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia</a>; public domain.</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (9)</summary><ul><li>stanza 2 is the refrain, sung after each of the other stanzas; the source gives it once.</li><li>line 3.1: 'zuckt es' = something flashes or jolts (a shudder, a flash of feeling) through them.</li><li>line 3.4: 'Landesmark' = the march, the border land of the country.</li><li>line 4.1: 'Himmelsau'n' = the meadows (fields) of heaven.</li><li>line 4.3: 'Kampfeslust' = eagerness for battle, delight in fighting.</li><li>line 5.3: 'ein Welscher' = a Welsh (Romance-speaking) foreigner, here a Frenchman; 'Welshman' keeps the old word's sense of 'foreigner' and is not the people of Wales.</li><li>line 6.2: 'Degen' = sword (a straight thrusting sword).</li><li>line 6.3: 'die Büchse spannt' = cocks (draws back the lock of) the gun.</li><li>line 7.4: 'wollen ... sein' = want to be, will be.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
